\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Cross-environment evidence.}
Does routing signal persist without catastrophic failure? On MATH Level~4--5 (300 problems), using a code interpreter as the deterministic modality, internal reasoning directionally outperforms: 81.0\% vs.\ 76.7\% accuracy ($+4.3$~pp, 95\% CI $[0.0, +8.7]$; McNemar $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). The label distribution inverts: 30 internal-preferred vs.\ 17 deterministic-preferred (cf.\ 9:86 in ScienceWorld), with 84.3\% no-preference. In the absence of death risk, the deterministic advantage vanishes---consistent with death avoidance as the primary driver. A complementary evaluation on ALFWorld ($N{=}135$) confirms that the counterfactual framework identifies different routing mechanisms across environments: the deterministic advantage there stems from information asymmetry (access to admissible actions) rather than death avoidance (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-env}).

