The $r = 0.97$ correlation is specific to catastrophic death (score $= -100$); defining ``failure'' as any non-positive score yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$). The correlation emerges only at $P \geq 50$ ($r = 0.86$) and reflects that non-death episodes show near-zero performance differential ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$; Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Death is effectively the \emph{only} component generating differential routing signal in ScienceWorld. These findings may be capability-dependent: the 42.9\% internal death rate reflects a 7B model's hallucination vulnerability; larger models with lower death rates would reduce DAF, potentially revealing non-death routing signal currently masked by catastrophic failures.

\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
The dominance of death avoidance (DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$) reframes modality routing as primarily a \emph{safety} problem rather than an efficiency-quality tradeoff. The router functions as a safety critic---predicting which states lead to catastrophic failure under internal reasoning---aligning more closely with safe RL~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive} than with cost-quality optimization~\citep{cogrouter-2026, ares-2026}. The extreme label imbalance (86:9 deterministic-preferred, 150 no-preference) is not an artifact but a central result: among episodes where modality matters, deterministic is preferred at 9.6:1, explaining why always-deterministic is a strong baseline and why the trained router (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) adds no value. A lightweight death predictor may capture most routing benefit without full counterfactual labeling.
